hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to  another edition of ask Evan in today's  episode I'm going to answer questions  from one of my readers Eric who runs a  software company has got 30 different  applications that are all in kind of  beta mode haven't really launched yet  and he's looking for a CEO to come in  and help him run the company wondered if  a CEO would buy in and that'd be a good  way to raise capital he's still working  at a full-time job and is you know has  it has a troubled a lot of entrepreneurs  go through where they need money to run  the business but they don't have enough  money coming in from the business to  kind of keep it going so looking for  ways to raise capital and also looking  at how to get these companies is  software company off the ground so to  give you some advice Eric first of all  it's very rare that CEOs will buy in  especially the kind of really early  stage you can get a CEO to come in and  join the company but unless you're  making sales it's hard for them to give  you money up front if you were you know  full time in this business and you  already had some traction really making  sales and there's a valuation on the  business it's a lot easier to convince  somebody to put money in at this stage  if you're not even in the business full  time it's hard to convince somebody to  put money in and join the company full  time to pay you a salary so you have you  have to get the business a little bit  further ahead if you want somebody to  put money into the company and join a  CEO so what I would suggest for you is  if you don't have the skill sets to be  the CEO and be the business development  guy then you can find somebody who can  join you in that role but they don't  probably wouldn't buy in but they could  join as an equity partner in the company  and make them earn in to the business so  when I had my first software company I  wasn't the founder I joined a couple  years after the company was started but  I became an owner in the business we  were one of three owners and I joined as  business development guy so I was out  doing media discipleship in partnership  relationships getting distribution  channel set up that was my role so I  believe in the company they already had  a little  bit of a track record so I joined and I  became you know chief operating officer  and one of the equity owners in the  business I didn't buy into the business  as it was in a cash outlay at the  beginning but it was my time invested to  you know help make that company grow so  you can still make that work you have to  find the right person obviously I try to  make them earn in so you want to be very  careful with money that you know equity  that you give up in your company you  it's like getting married when you get  partners in your business so you want to  make sure that there's milestones that  are put in there so they don't just earn  whatever you're thinking giving up  twenty-five percent thirty percent fifty  percent you don't just give it them  around the bat you have to prove  themselves so it could be you know  making X number of sales or whatever you  want them to do as part of their job  description make them earn in make sure  they earn your trust make sure something  you can work with for a long period of  time and that could be an option for you  to go if you're really struggling on the  money side you have two options one is  keep going part time and focus on one of  the things that really will help grow  your business they said he had 30  applications what's the one that's  closest to actually be able to make some  cash alright the cash flow you got to  get it coming in if you want to be able  to spend more time with your business  there's nothing wrong with doing it part  time it just takes longer a lot of  businesses are started part-time  moonlighting until you get to the point  where you can afford to spend more time  on it it's a great way to be able to  build a business it's not the sexy way  but it's a great way to be able to build  your business but I would focus your  efforts on set of 30 programs pick one  that you think you can get some money  and quickly with and build that one out  the second option is to go out and raise  capital and it could be from an investor  you're looking probably in an angel  investor at this stage the challenge  here is if you're not in a full-time  it's going to be hard to convince an  investor to give you money for and if  your pre sales or you just have a little  bit of sales you're going to get killed  on the valuation so you might get the  money you need but you might give up a  huge chunk of your company where if you  let it wait a little bit longer and you  had  more sales and more revenue coming in  you could get a much better valuation so  Erica hope that helps a lot of  information I would still go out and try  to find somebody if you don't think you  have the skills to help you run the  business make sure that they do burn in  and there's nothing wrong with keep  going part-time just focus on getting  the software you know the one program  out there that will help generate some  revenue for your business so you can  afford to leave your job and run your  business on a full-time basis and if you  really need to go up raise capital but  maybe make it in small chunks you're not  giving up too much of the business I  hope that helps let me know what you  guys think if you wanna leave a comment  underneath the video love to hear what  your experience has been or if you have  other questions or so my device for Eric  as well and if you like the video please  give it a thumbs up I always appreciate  seeing those come in it makes you want  to do more of these videos so thank you  and we'll see you on the next episode  you
 

